  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CAPACITY BUILDING FOR MONITORING &amp; CONS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Veracruz state is one of the most diverse regions in Mexico. Each year about 220 different migratory species travel along the Gulf of Mxico to use its different habitats as stopover sites or wintering grounds. Information about these species and how they use habitat is scarce and concentrated in a few locations. The main threats to migratory species are loss and fragmentation of wintering and stopover habitat, climate change, pollution, predation by domestic cats, and window collision. This project aims to promote citizen science monitoring efforts to broaden our understanding of how migratory birds use a variety of conserved and disturbed habitats as well as citizen empowerment to direct habitat management and restoration actions in Veracruz. The North American Bird Conservation Initiative (NABCI), based at the National Commission for the Knowledge and Use of Biodiversity (Comisin Nacional para el Conocimiento y Uso de la Biodiversidad CONABIO) has a 12 year history developing citizen science projects oriented to involve people in bird monitoring and bird conservation actions throughout Mexico. This grant will help further develop the NABCI-CONABIO projects by expanding local capabilities for monitoring and conservation; generating relevant data and knowledge about Neotropical migratory species conservation needs; establishing citizen science projects in the region; and implementing outreach.</narrative>
  </description>
